{"attack": true, "case": "The target is internally self-contradictory: it asserts 'the only consistent Popperian stance is radical skepticism where no theory is preferred for action over another UNLESS one has been falsified and the other has not.' But the target's own mechanism declares that 'any move from has survived to should be used entails a premise that the future will resemble the past,' which is induction. Preferring a non-falsified theory over a falsified one is itself a move from a historical fact ('has been falsified') to a normative action directive ('should avoid this theory going forward'). By the target's own stated criterion, this residual preference smuggles in exactly the same inductive premise it condemns. The carve-out ('unless one has been falsified and the other has not') is a preference-for-action that treats past falsification as a reason for future action—the very structure the target identifies as necessarily inductive. Therefore the target cannot coherently propose even this 'radical skeptical' stance as the sole consistent position, because its own logic brands that stance inconsistent as well. The target either must endorse total paralysis (no preference at all, including not preferring unfalsified over falsified), or it concedes that some non-inductive methodological preferences are possible—collapsing the entire argument.", "premise": "The target's mechanism states that ANY move from a theory's historical status ('has survived' / 'has been falsified') to a normative action directive ('should be used' / 'should be avoided') necessarily smuggles in the inductive premise that the future will resemble the past. The target's proposed 'consistent' stance carves out an exception allowing preference for the non-falsified theory over the falsified one, which is itself a move from historical status to action directive.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "74b03f7c1e846cd83c3f8a56cbf026c3522a89fa848b808e445d036ed893909d", "quote": "the only consistent Popperian stance is radical skepticism where no theory is preferred for action over another unless one has been falsified and the other has not."}, {"block": "74b03f7c1e846cd83c3f8a56cbf026c3522a89fa848b808e445d036ed893909d", "quote": "Any move from 'has survived' to 'should be used' entails a premise that 'the future will resemble the past' or 'nature is uniform,' which is the definition of induction."}], "counterexample": [{"scenario": "Two theories T1 and T2 exist. T1 has been falsified by a past observation; T2 has not. The target says we should prefer T2 for action. But by its own mechanism, this preference is a move from a historical fact (T1 'has been falsified') to an action directive ('should avoid T1 / prefer T2'), which the target says necessarily smuggles in induction. The target's 'consistent' stance is therefore inconsistent by its own criterion."}], "successor_question": "If the target's residual preference (unfalsified over falsified) also smuggles in induction by its own stated logic, what stance remains that is both non-inductive and non-paralytic, and does that remaining stance require a methodological justification of the very kind the target denies exists?"}